7cbab487-bc20-43fc-889b-653b9e6aa395	38f12f20-1f22-46a1-80a8-05ea80b82966	9368a037-87e3-4be5-92e8-1549f814baec	2	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem on screen:** LeetCode 1, *Two Sum*. Given an integer array `nums` and an integer `target`, return the indices of the two numbers that add up to `target`. Exactly one solution exists, and the same element can't be used twice. The answer can be in any order. The editor is set to Python3 with an empty `twoSum` stub.\n\n**Solution (one-pass hash map):**\n\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def twoSum(self, nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\n        seen = {}  # value -> index\n        for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n            need = target - x\n            if need in seen:\n                return [seen[need], i]\n            seen[x] = i\n        return []\n```\n\n**Approach:** Go through the array once. For each number `x`, check whether `target - x` is already in the dictionary. If it is, return that index and the current one. If not, store `x` with its index. Checking before storing means the same element is never used twice. It also handles duplicates like `[3,3]` with target 6, which returns `[0,1]`.\n\n**Complexity:**\n- Time: O(n), with one pass and O(1) average dictionary lookups.\n- Space: O(n) for the dictionary.\n\n**Checked against the examples:**\n- `[2,7,11,15]`, 9 → `[0,1]`\n- `[3,2,4]`, 6 → `[1,2]`\n- `[3,3]`, 6 → `[0,1]`\n\nThe constraint is `2 <= nums.length <= 10^4`, so O(n) is more than fast enough. A brute-force O(n²) double loop would also pass at that size, but the hash map is the expected answer.	claude-sonnet-5-5	1035	546	5383	{}	2026-09-30 04:30:58.414209+00	2026-09-30 04:30:58.669264+00
