d4675290-3c2f-4f7d-b12c-06282819b1f5	801f5e53-66b0-4153-875e-11e695ec6a03	26b2bb07-bb2b-4f91-8a0e-6c9408ec3dbb	4	assistant	agent	chat	\N	**O(1)** means **constant time** (or constant space): the work an operation takes doesn't grow with the input size. Whether the input has 10 items or 10 million, it takes roughly the same number of steps.\n\n**Examples:**\n- Accessing an array/list element by index: `arr[5]`\n- Looking up, inserting, or deleting a key in a hash map/dict or set (on average)\n- Pushing/popping from a stack\n\n**Relevance to your LeetCode problem (Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters):**\n- Each set or dict lookup while scanning the string is O(1) on average, which is what makes the sliding-window solution run in O(n) total.\n- Space is technically O(min(n, k)), where k is the alphabet size. If the characters are limited to a fixed set (e.g., ASCII), that is often described as O(1) space.\n\n**Quick solution for reference:**\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def lengthOfLongestSubstring(self, s: str) -> int:\n        last = {}          # char -> last index seen\n        start = best = 0\n        for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n            if ch in last and last[ch] >= start:\n                start = last[ch] + 1\n            last[ch] = i\n            best = max(best, i - start + 1)\n        return best\n```\nTime: O(n). Space: O(min(n, k)).	claude-sonnet-5-5	880	442	4043	{}	2026-09-30 05:06:50.785272+00	2026-09-30 05:06:50.669319+00
