60c07697-4012-4ad2-abdd-510f158d363b	30b73430-f830-40ec-8734-d4b3aafbde19	f30fe0fa-46ab-4447-b360-87f26ac1e2f6	2	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** LeetCode 5, Longest Palindromic Substring. Given a string `s` (length 1–1000, digits and English letters), return the longest palindromic substring. Example: `"babad"` → `"bab"` or `"aba"`; `"cbbd"` → `"bb"`.\n\n**Solution (expand around center, Python):**\n\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def longestPalindrome(self, s: str) -> str:\n        start, end = 0, 0\n\n        def expand(l: int, r: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n            while l >= 0 and r < len(s) and s[l] == s[r]:\n                l -= 1\n                r += 1\n            return l + 1, r - 1  # bounds of the palindrome\n\n        for i in range(len(s)):\n            l1, r1 = expand(i, i)       # odd length\n            l2, r2 = expand(i, i + 1)   # even length\n            if r1 - l1 > end - start:\n                start, end = l1, r1\n            if r2 - l2 > end - start:\n                start, end = l2, r2\n\n        return s[start:end + 1]\n```\n\n**Approach:** Every palindrome mirrors around a center. There are 2n−1 centers: n single characters for odd lengths and n−1 gaps between characters for even lengths. For each center, expand outward while the characters match, and track the longest range found.\n\n**Complexity:**\n- Time: O(n²). There are O(n) centers and each expansion is O(n) in the worst case. With n ≤ 1000, that is about 10⁶ operations, which is fine.\n- Space: O(1) extra, apart from the returned substring.\n\n**Alternative:** Manacher's algorithm solves it in O(n) time, but it is usually unnecessary for this problem. Expand-around-center is the standard interview answer.	claude-sonnet-5-5	1125	596	25964	{}	2026-09-30 06:10:02.829391+00	2026-09-30 06:10:02.955525+00
