91eb7442-752e-487b-914b-18927edce00c	30b73430-f830-40ec-8734-d4b3aafbde19	f4d7ccbf-a4da-407c-8ce7-eff47048ee40	36	assistant	agent	conversor	\N	**Question:** What's the difference between the time and space complexity of the different approaches?\n\n**Suggested answer:**\n\nGood question. Let me clarify. Time complexity tells you how many operations the algorithm performs as the input grows—for instance, expand-around-center does O(n²) comparisons in the worst case because you check n different centers and each can expand up to n characters. Space complexity is how much extra memory you use beyond the input. Expand-around-center uses O(1) space—just a couple of variables to track positions. Dynamic programming, on the other hand, uses O(n²) space because you build a full table with n² cells to mark which substrings are palindromes, even though the time is also O(n²). So you're trading memory for potentially clearer logic. Manacher's algorithm flips this: it gets O(n) time but needs O(n) extra space to track the centers and radii. So when choosing an approach, you ask: Do I have memory constraints? Then expand-around-center. Do I need the absolute fastest time and memory isn't a concern? Then Manacher's. For most interviews, expand-around-center is the practical sweet spot.	claude-haiku-4-5-20251001	2794	282	3617	{"roles": {"interviewee": "mic", "interviewer": "unknown"}}	2026-09-30 06:13:24.075859+00	2026-09-30 06:13:24.050494+00
