6a3e7986-5acc-4dd5-a5e9-dc9d1439133e	45b2c36d-1a8e-497d-9281-352e190107df	\N	39	user	screen	\N	\N	vy — & Romantointeger-LeetCode xX + > Ask Gemini | a x\n€ 2 CG AM 2% leetcode.com/problems/roman-to-integer/description/ @MaskGoogle vy Th @ :\ngo © Mail & Social_Media 9) Prog.Reources (4) Journals (CQ Torrents @ Order food online fr... MJ] http://127.0.0.1:8050/ x wxPython 101: Usin... Python Based HMI Energy Meter Logg... » (© All Bookmarks\n6 ProblemList < > 26 GP @®submt O # % 8 & 60 6 & @ Premium\n& Description Editorial A Solutions —D Submissions t1 < </> Code\nPython3 ¥  @ Auto 2EAQO9O\n13. Roman to Integer , fais 5\n2 | def romanToInt(self, s: str) -> int:\nEasy © Topics & Companies @ Hint 3\n\nRoman numerals are represented by seven different symbols: I, v, X, L, C, D and M.\n\nSymbol Value\n1\n\n5\n\n10\n50\n100\n\n500\n1000 Testcase >_ Test Result\n\nZ0o0rx<H\n\nFor example, 2 is written as II in Roman numeral, just two ones added together. 12 is written as XII, which is\nsimply X + II. The number 27 is written as XxVII, which is XX + V + II.\n\nRoman numerals are usually written largest to smallest from left to right. However, the numeral for four is not\nTILL. Instead, the number four is written as IV. Because the one is before the five we subtract it making four. The\nsame principle applies to the number nine, which is written as 1x. There are six instances where subtraction is\nused:\n\n* I canbe placed before v (5) and x (10) to make 4 and 9.\n\n* x canbe placed before L (50) and ¢ (100) to make 40 and 90.\n\n* canbe placed before (500) and m (1000) to make 400 and 900. ’\n\n82K FB O70 w 4 @ © 143 Online\n32°C -— 0] ae ENG 17:36\nMostly sunny Gm Q Search (aa L qj al g | =} 8 Was) © F @ & @ A 2 Ys FM ® or 40-2026	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	2026-10-01 12:06:43.776884+00	2026-10-01 12:06:43.780906+00
