92d644d1-0595-43b5-8a9c-f22c0e49cbbf	45b2c36d-1a8e-497d-9281-352e190107df	6a3e7986-5acc-4dd5-a5e9-dc9d1439133e	41	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** Convert a Roman numeral string `s` (valid, 1 to 3999) to an integer, handling subtractive pairs like IV and IX.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. Map each symbol to its value\n2. total = 0\n3. For each index i in s:\n4.   If a next char exists and value[s[i]] < value[s[i+1]]: subtract value[s[i]]\n5.   Else: add value[s[i]]\n6. Return total\n```\n\n**Code:**\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def romanToInt(self, s: str) -> int:\n        v = {"I": 1, "V": 5, "X": 10, "L": 50,\n             "C": 100, "D": 500, "M": 1000}\n        total = 0\n        for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n            # a smaller value before a larger one is subtractive\n            if i + 1 < len(s) and v[ch] < v[s[i + 1]]:\n                total -= v[ch]\n            else:\n                total += v[ch]\n        return total\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: A symbol is subtracted only when a larger symbol follows it.\n- Why correct: Each symbol's sign depends only on its right neighbor, covering all six subtractive forms.\n- Edge cases: single char "I", "MCMXCIV", "III", last character always added, all handled.\n- Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space; brute force that matches substrings is also O(n) but clumsier.\n\n**Alternate method:** Right-to-left scan keeping the previous value, O(n)/O(1); avoids the index bounds check but is less intuitive to read.	claude-sonnet-5-5	4191	540	5217	{}	2026-10-01 12:06:48.995349+00	2026-10-01 12:06:49.000389+00
