127c68ce-f0ae-45ad-84d0-b78a534897ed	d0649a8d-9950-4f12-8de8-81279542c57f	\N	1	user	manual	\N	\N	Larkspur Health — Patient Voice & Chat Agent Design (AI-Native Engineer, Design Track)\nThe scenario\nYou are joining Larkspur Health, a seed-stage startup that builds voice and chat agents that talk to patients on behalf of clinics. Larkspur's first customer is Saguaro Family Medicine.\n\n1. Context\nSaguaro runs fourteen primary care clinics: eight in Arizona (Phoenix and Tucson), four in Nevada (Las Vegas) and two in New Mexico (Albuquerque). It has about 75,000 active patients, and a little over 40% of them are on Medicare or a Medicare Advantage plan.\n\nAbout 30% of Saguaro's patients list Spanish as their preferred language. Some clinics have bilingual front desk staff. Most do not.\n\nMaya Ortiz, Larkspur's co-founder, in her own words:\n\n"Saguaro's phone lines are drowning. They take around 2,800 calls a day and Monday morning hold times are over twenty minutes. After 5pm everything goes to an answering service that pages the on-call doctor for things a nurse could handle, and misses things it shouldn't. We want one agent that answers the phone, the text line and the chat on the patient portal. It should book and move appointments, take refill requests, answer the 'is my MRI approved yet' and 'do you take my plan' questions, and make the follow-up calls Saguaro owes patients within two business days of a hospital discharge. I'd love it to tell patients whether they need to come in. Saguaro's privacy officer asked me exactly one question in our last meeting: where does the audio go? Their front desk lives in Epic. We go live at one clinic in eight weeks."\n\n2. What comes in\nFive recent contacts, with names and details changed:\n\nA. Phone, Las Vegas clinic line, Monday 7:52am. "I need my lisinopril refilled. And my insurance changed on January 1st, I'm on a Humana plan now. Do I need to do anything?"\nB. Portal chat, Thursday 11:40pm. "my dad was discharged from Desert Valley Medical Center yesterday for his heart failure. tonight he's more short of breath than this morning and his ankles look bigger. should I be worried"\nC. Phone, Tucson clinic line, Tuesday 2:15pm. "Hola, quiero saber si ya aprobaron la resonancia de mi esposo. El doctor la pidió hace dos semanas."\nD. Text, from a number not on file. "this is Dana Whitfield DOB 3/14/1961 can you text me my A1C results"\nE. Phone, from a 612 area code, February. "I'm down here for the winter, staying in Mesa, 85206. Can somebody see me this week for a cough that won't go away? I've never been to you before."\nEach of these needs something different. Working out what, and what the agent should and should not do on its own, is part of the job.\n\n3. Your task\nThis is a design exercise. Do not write code. You have 40 minutes: roughly 10 on questions, 10 on assumptions and 20 on the design, though the split is yours.\n\nYou have not been told everything you need, on purpose. Nobody is available to answer questions while you work. Produce your questions, your assumptions and your solution design, in that order, in APPROACH.md (Section 4).\n\n4. APPROACH.md\nAPPROACH.md is the one document a reviewer reads. Put your diagram in a separate file and link it from APPROACH.md. Anything not reachable from APPROACH.md will not be read. Short notes are fine: we read for decisions and the reasons behind them, and anything past two pages of design is not read. Cover these sections, in this order:\n\nSection A — Questions. What you would ask Maya, Saguaro, or anyone else before building this. Up to 12, most important first. For each, one line on what the answer would change in your design.\nSection B — Assumptions. Where you cannot wait for an answer, the assumptions you are making so you can move forward. For each: the assumption, your reason for it, and what changes if it turns out to be wrong.\nSection C — Solution design. One page, plus one diagram. How the agent works across phone, text and chat. What it does on its own and what it hands to a person, and how. How it connects to Saguaro's systems. How contacts A to E move through it. Name a specific product or vendor only where the choice matters, and say why.\nSection D — Eight-week scope. What you would ship at the one clinic in eight weeks, and what you would leave for later.\nSection E — Next step. Only if you run out of time: one line on what you would have done next.\n5. Deliverables\nAPPROACH.md, with Sections A to E.\nOne diagram, linked from Section C of APPROACH.md. It can be a tool export or a photo of a hand sketch.\nSubmit within the 40 minutes. If you run out of time, submit what you have, with Section E filled in.\n\n6. Working on the platform\nDo all of your work on the platform, from your first note to your submitted APPROACH.md. You are free to use the AI agent there for anything that helps: thinking the problem through, brainstorming, exploring options, checking your ideas, drafting. The platform keeps a record of your prompts and AI conversations, and that record is part of what we review. You do not need to paste it into APPROACH.md or tidy it up.\n\nBring your own thinking. Do not paste in prompts, answers or text prepared elsewhere. The platform records the full session, so pasted-in material and work done off the platform are visible to us, and we treat them as an integrity breach.	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	2026-10-28 12:57:36.127571+00	2026-10-28 12:57:36.140668+00
