fe543a8b-e27d-4b8e-b5c5-4b7db4fda455	c362e595-f5b9-47de-8635-16fd3b9ec936	aecb662b-c822-450b-aea8-8010c372449f	2	assistant	agent	conversor	\N	**Question: How to deploy an application to Kubernetes.**\n\nHere's a strong answer:\n\n"Deploying to Kubernetes involves a few key steps. First, you containerize your application—typically with Docker—to create a container image. Then you push that image to a container registry like Docker Hub or your cloud provider's registry. Next, you write Kubernetes manifests, usually YAML files, that define your Deployments, Services, and any other resources your app needs. A Deployment specifies the container image, replicas, resource requests, and environment variables. You apply these manifests to your cluster using `kubectl apply -f`, and Kubernetes handles orchestration—scheduling pods, managing replicas, and handling rollouts. For production, I'd also consider setting up persistent volumes if you need state, configuring ingress for external traffic, and using namespaces to organize resources. Most teams use CI/CD pipelines to automate this so deployments happen on each code commit."\n\n---\n*Note: Without knowing your specific experience or the role context, I've given a general overview. If you have real projects using Kubernetes, anchor this with concrete details (e.g., "I deployed a Node.js microservice to EKS with 3 replicas").*	claude-haiku-4-5-20251001	860	288	4214	{"roles": {"interviewee": "mic", "interviewer": "unknown"}}	2026-10-08 08:30:35.795687+00	2026-10-08 08:30:36.218926+00
