1d98f0cd-91a3-4bbc-87cf-9bdec717a611	904a66ec-2102-4b9b-b8b1-a83837b98eb5	cc2417c2-9c5a-4a84-8afc-a66f6906d6ac	262	assistant	agent	conversor	\N	**Question:** What pattern or architecture did you follow in building this AI agent solution?\n\n**Suggested answer:**\n\nSo for the architecture, I followed an agentic loop pattern. The workflow is essentially a cycle with multiple specialized agents—a summary agent that processes the GST notices and PDFs, an action plan agent that determines what needs to be done, and a reply agent that generates the response to the GST officer.\n\nThe key part is the human-in-the-loop component. After the reply agent generates a response, it goes to a critic agent for review, which provides feedback. The user can then re-prompt to modify the output, and it loops back. This continues until they're satisfied, and then it moves to the approval flow.\n\nI chose this pattern because it allows for iterative refinement—the AI generates content, gets reviewed, and the user has control to steer it in the right direction. It also reduces hallucinations since there's human oversight at each stage, which is critical for compliance-related documents like GST notices. So that's why I went with the loop-based agentic architecture rather than a single-pass approach.	claude-haiku-4-5-20251001	4944	263	4538	{"roles": {"interviewee": "mic", "interviewer": "loopback"}}	2026-10-08 12:24:14.754053+00	2026-10-08 14:34:44.325097+00
