29dffc0f-664b-4360-9172-94ab4537d2ed	dd95af7c-f247-464d-9525-e7586117b032	e59a525b-94b6-4a5f-849a-d2e2e28630fa	210	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** Sliding-window average of size k. OCR dropped commas, so I read a as [2,3,1,4,5,7,8,1] and k as 2, which gives 2.5, 2, 2.5, 4.5, ... as shown.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. If k <= 0 or k > len(a): return []\n2. s = sum of first k elements; result = [s/k]\n3. For i from k to n-1:\n4.     s += a[i] - a[i-k]   (slide window)\n5.     append s/k\n6. Return result\n```\n\n**Code:**\n```python\ndef moving_avg(a, k):\n    if k <= 0 or k > len(a):\n        return []\n    s = sum(a[:k])\n    res = [s / k]\n    for i in range(k, len(a)):\n        s += a[i] - a[i - k]  # add new element, drop the oldest\n        res.append(s / k)\n    return res\n\na = [2, 3, 1, 4, 5, 7, 8, 1]\nprint(moving_avg(a, 2))  # [2.5, 2.0, 2.5, 4.5, 6.0, 7.5, 4.5]\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: Keep a running window sum, adding the new element and dropping the oldest.\n- Why correct: After each step, `s` equals the sum of the current k elements.\n- Edge cases: empty array, k > n, k <= 0, k = 1, k = n, negatives, all handled.\n- Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) extra space; brute force re-summing each window costs O(n·k).\n\n**Alternate method:** Prefix sums, O(n) time and O(n) space; simpler for range queries but uses extra memory.	claude-sonnet-5-5	4644	1332	10567	{}	2026-10-21 11:22:22.504138+00	2026-10-21 11:34:14.196209+00
