3d2b6c97-00e9-48c3-af46-d8b18f25b6c5	205abf2e-a091-4839-9a25-29b868d1b207	183ee4f8-5276-45ff-811f-1c62ccd34655	4	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** LeetCode 35: in a sorted array of distinct integers, return the index of `target`, or the index where it would be inserted, in O(log n).\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. lo = 0, hi = n (hi is exclusive)\n2. while lo < hi:\n3.   mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2\n4.   if nums[mid] < target: lo = mid + 1\n5.   else: hi = mid\n6. return lo (first index with nums[i] >= target)\n```\n\n**Code:** (C++, as shown on screen)\n```cpp\nclass Solution {\npublic:\n    int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n        int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2; // avoids int overflow\n            if (nums[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;\n            else hi = mid;\n        }\n        return lo; // lower bound: first index with nums[i] >= target\n    }\n};\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: Find the lower bound, the first index whose value is ≥ target.\n- Why correct: Everything left of `lo` is < target; everything from `hi` onward is ≥ target.\n- Edge cases: empty (returns 0), single element, target smaller than all, larger than all (returns n), overflow: handled.\n- Complexity: O(log n) time, O(1) space; a linear scan would be O(n).\n\n**Alternate method:** `std::lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target) - nums.begin()` is O(log n) time and O(1) space. It is shorter but hides the logic, so interviewers usually want the manual loop.	claude-sonnet-5-5	1742	570	4886	{}	2026-10-23 07:57:14.780886+00	2026-10-23 07:57:15.938667+00
