c4d39dfb-0b57-4e9d-8230-80021dd2dfbd	b46fa237-ba67-40a9-a1c3-231a9778c6e3	7733c929-c5d7-455f-8c11-b6621689e57f	8	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** Given `height` of length n, pick two lines that maximize `min(h[i], h[j]) * (j - i)`; return that area (LeetCode 11, C++).\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. l = 0, r = n-1, best = 0\n2. while l < r:\n3.   area = min(h[l], h[r]) * (r - l); best = max(best, area)\n4.   move the pointer at the shorter line inward\n5. return best\n```\n\n**Code:**\n```cpp\nclass Solution {\npublic:\n    int maxArea(vector<int>& height) {\n        int l = 0, r = (int)height.size() - 1, best = 0;\n        while (l < r) {\n            best = max(best, min(height[l], height[r]) * (r - l));\n            // the shorter line limits the area, so only moving it can help\n            if (height[l] < height[r]) l++;\n            else r--;\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: Width shrinks each step, so only moving the shorter line can raise the height.\n- Why correct: Every discarded pair involves the shorter line and cannot beat the area already recorded.\n- Edge cases: n=2, zeros, equal heights, strictly sorted arrays all handled; max area is about 1e9, which fits in int.\n- Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space; brute force over all pairs is O(n²).\n\n**Alternate method:** Brute force over all pairs, O(n²) time and O(1) space; simpler but too slow for n up to 10⁵.	claude-sonnet-5-5	2394	512	4667	{}	2026-10-24 17:30:24.642021+00	2026-10-24 17:30:25.184717+00
