d2f0fd80-9268-43a8-b527-b86e081cce32	37ed911e-34d2-48c8-94d2-9ddced0e1e94	994717d6-997f-4949-89fb-cbeff893bea8	140	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** Define one variable of each built-in Python data type in the editor. The screen shows `c` reused for the str and the bool, and `tup_1=` left unfinished.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. Numeric: int, float, complex\n2. Text and boolean: str, bool\n3. Sequences: list, tuple, range\n4. Mapping and sets: dict, set, frozenset\n5. Binary: bytes, bytearray, memoryview; plus None\n6. Print type() of each to verify\n```\n\n**Code:**\n```python\ni = 10\nf = 3.14\nc = 2 + 3j\ns = "hello"            # was 'c' on screen; renamed so c stays complex\nb = True               # was 'c' again; renamed\nlst = [1, 2, 3, 4]\ntup_1 = (1, 2, 3, 4)\nrng = range(5)\ndict_1 = {"name": "aaa", "type": "xx"}\nst = {1, 2, 3}\nfs = frozenset({1, 2})\nby = b"abc"\nba = bytearray(b"abc")\nmv = memoryview(by)\nn = None\n\nfor v in (i, f, c, s, b, lst, tup_1, rng, dict_1, st, fs, by, ba, mv, n):\n    print(type(v).__name__)\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: Python infers the type from the assigned literal, so no declaration is needed.\n- Why correct: each name is unique, so no variable overwrites another.\n- Edge cases: the empty set needs `set()` because `{}` is a dict; `(1)` is an int, so a single-element tuple needs `(1,)`.\n- Complexity: O(1) time and space; there is no brute-force alternative.\n\n**Alternate method:** Put the values in one dict, `{type(v).__name__: v for v in values}`, which is more compact but less explicit than one variable per type.	claude-sonnet-5-5	4933	830	7574	{}	2026-10-27 09:43:14.592465+00	2026-10-27 09:43:15.267939+00
