7cd38656-e7f4-4934-90fe-e4df98fc9a0c	37ed911e-34d2-48c8-94d2-9ddced0e1e94	4e47dea1-533f-430c-aee3-edcef9c49ad7	257	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** Finish the on-screen `change_op` decorator so `add(a, b)` can subtract, multiply, etc. without editing its body.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. change_op(op) takes the replacement operation\n2. deco(func) receives the original function\n3. wrapper(a, b) returns op(a, b) in place of func's own logic\n4. deco returns wrapper; change_op returns deco\n5. Put @change_op(...) above add\n6. Call add(1, 2) and print the result\n```\n\n**Code:**\n```python\nfrom functools import wraps\n\ndef change_op(op):\n    def deco(func):\n        @wraps(func)  # keeps add's name and docstring\n        def wrapper(a, b):\n            return op(a, b)\n        return wrapper\n    return deco\n\n@change_op(lambda a, b: a - b)\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(1, 2))  # -1\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: a decorator factory swaps behaviour while `add`'s body stays untouched.\n- Why correct: `@` rebinds `add` to `wrapper`, which applies the injected `op`.\n- Edge cases: other operators (`*`, `/`) just need a different lambda; division by zero is not handled.\n- Complexity: O(1) time and space per call; brute force means editing every function by hand.\n\n**Alternate method:** Pass the operation as a parameter, `def calc(a, b, op=operator.add)` (O(1)); simpler, but it changes the function signature.	claude-sonnet-5-5	5163	893	7226	{}	2026-10-27 09:50:47.461488+00	2026-10-27 09:50:47.27953+00
