9cf53249-39c0-4510-8548-a991c23ec7f4	f022a035-22db-48ae-8763-f72c4f0a9129	a979287d-a854-4c78-9923-b8126b41f40b	81	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** Return the sorted distinct tiles reachable after one roll (1..die_sides) from start_tile. Cap at N, then apply at most one teleport from the "from,to" map. The example (`["3,1","4,2","5,10"]`, die 6, start 0, N 12) gives `[1, 2, 6, 10]`.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. Parse each "from,to" string into dict tp[from] = to\n2. seen = empty set\n3. for r in 1..die_sides:\n4.   t = min(start_tile + r, N)\n5.   if t in tp: t = tp[t]   (no second teleport)\n6.   add t to seen\n7. return sorted(seen)\n```\n\n**Code:**\n```python\ndef destinations(teleporters, die_sides, start_tile, N):\n    tp = {}\n    for s in teleporters:\n        a, b = s.split(",")\n        tp[int(a)] = int(b)\n    seen = set()\n    for r in range(1, die_sides + 1):\n        t = start_tile + r\n        if t > N:\n            t = N  # hard cap at the final tile\n        if t in tp:\n            t = tp[t]  # single teleport only\n        seen.add(t)\n    return sorted(seen)\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: a dict gives O(1) teleport lookup, and a set removes duplicate destinations.\n- Why correct: each roll is capped once, then teleported at most once, never chained.\n- Edge cases: no teleporters, cap at N, teleport onto a teleporter tile, duplicate "from" entries (last wins); handled.\n- Complexity: O(die_sides + T + k log k) time, O(T + die_sides) space; brute force scans T per roll, O(die_sides·T).\n\n**Alternate method:** Loop only to min(die_sides, N − start_tile) and handle tile N once for all capped rolls. That is the same big-O, but faster when N is near the start.	claude-sonnet-5-5	5667	1023	7343	{}	2026-10-28 07:25:01.518278+00	2026-10-28 07:25:02.563683+00
