808127c9-e351-4f40-80fb-2685139fbfc2	03c687bf-0f6d-47ab-9f40-dcd4caa81525	78966abd-6ea7-475f-bc6a-7fc88bd02cef	454	assistant	agent	conversor	\N	Q: SQL query for the third-highest salary\n\n**I sort the distinct salaries in descending order, skip the top two, and take the next one.**\n\n```sql\n-- salaries 90k, 80k, 80k, 70k -> third highest = 70k\nSELECT DISTINCT amount FROM salary\nORDER BY amount DESC\nLIMIT 1 OFFSET 2;                         -- 70000\n\n-- with employee names, ties handled\nSELECT e.name, s.amount\nFROM (SELECT emp_id, amount,\n        DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY amount DESC) AS rnk\n      FROM salary) s\nJOIN employee e ON e.emp_id = s.emp_id\nWHERE s.rnk = 3;\n```\n\n- `DISTINCT` and `DENSE_RANK` count tied salaries only once, so two people at 80k don't push the real third value out.\n- In SQLAlchemy, the same query is `select(Salary.amount).distinct().order_by(Salary.amount.desc()).offset(2).limit(1)`.	claude-opus-5-5	8569	1603	6147	{"roles": {"interviewee": "mic", "interviewer": "loopback"}}	2026-10-29 07:07:09.044298+00	2026-10-29 07:07:10.240892+00
6bd430ea-0b57-472b-81ed-15f0611a18c4	03c687bf-0f6d-47ab-9f40-dcd4caa81525	\N	455	user	manual	\N	\N	orm query required	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	2026-10-29 07:07:26.015178+00	2026-10-29 07:07:25.615309+00
18c48fed-8e29-40fd-b4dd-4ca202f33391	03c687bf-0f6d-47ab-9f40-dcd4caa81525	6bd430ea-0b57-472b-81ed-15f0611a18c4	457	assistant	agent	chat	\N	**For the third-highest salary, I use a distinct, descending sort and skip two rows, in SQLAlchemy 2.0.**\n\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import ForeignKey, String, Numeric, select, func, create_engine\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase, Mapped, mapped_column, Session\n\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase):\n    pass\n\nclass Employee(Base):\n    __tablename__ = "employee"\n    emp_id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    name: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(100))\n\nclass Salary(Base):\n    __tablename__ = "salary"\n    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    emp_id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(ForeignKey("employee.emp_id"))\n    amount: Mapped[float] = mapped_column(Numeric(12, 2))\n\nengine = create_engine("sqlite:///:memory:")  # swap for your DB URL\nBase.metadata.create_all(engine)\n\nwith Session(engine) as session:\n    # 1) Third-highest salary value\n    stmt = (\n        select(Salary.amount)\n        .distinct()\n        .order_by(Salary.amount.desc())\n        .offset(2)\n        .limit(1)\n    )\n    third_highest = session.scalar(stmt)\n\n    # 2) Employees earning the third-highest salary (ties handled)\n    ranked = select(\n        Salary.emp_id,\n        Salary.amount,\n        func.dense_rank().over(order_by=Salary.amount.desc()).label("rnk"),\n    ).subquery()\n\n    stmt = (\n        select(Employee.name, ranked.c.amount)\n        .join(ranked, ranked.c.emp_id == Employee.emp_id)\n        .where(ranked.c.rnk == 3)\n    )\n    for name, amount in session.execute(stmt):\n        print(name, amount)\n```\n\n- **Query 1** returns only the value. `distinct()` stops duplicate salaries from taking up a rank, and it returns `None` if there are fewer than three distinct salaries.\n- **Query 2** uses `dense_rank()`, so everyone tied at the third-highest value is returned with their name.\n- SQLAlchemy sends the values as bound parameters, so there is no SQL injection risk.	claude-sonnet-5-5	5674	994	6629	{}	2026-10-29 07:07:32.645243+00	2026-10-29 07:07:31.809238+00
