87e25028-1602-4683-a09a-1db6c0eb512c	03c687bf-0f6d-47ab-9f40-dcd4caa81525	b881c3f8-cd69-493e-a228-40a6f795b4b4	468	assistant	agent	chat	\N	**Highest salary with the SQLAlchemy ORM:**\n\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import select, func\n\n# Just the value\nhighest = session.scalar(select(func.max(Salary.amount)))\nprint(highest)\n```\n\nAlternative without an aggregate:\n\n```python\nhighest = session.scalar(\n    select(Salary.amount).order_by(Salary.amount.desc()).limit(1)\n)\n```\n\nIf they also want the employee name:\n\n```python\nrow = session.execute(\n    select(Employee.name, Salary.amount)\n    .join(Salary, Salary.emp_id == Employee.emp_id)\n    .order_by(Salary.amount.desc())\n    .limit(1)\n).first()\n```\n\n- `func.max` runs `SELECT MAX(amount) FROM salary` in the database.\n- This assumes the same `Employee` and `Salary` models from the earlier answer.\n- The `limit(1)` version returns only one employee if there are ties. Use `where(Salary.amount == select(func.max(Salary.amount)).scalar_subquery())` to return all tied employees.	claude-sonnet-5-5	5754	358	3511	{}	2026-10-29 07:09:04.889988+00	2026-10-29 07:09:04.883868+00
