f6b8ec54-903c-4f05-ba59-9ab35e2f9627	03c687bf-0f6d-47ab-9f40-dcd4caa81525	d1f08dd8-6729-4bff-8bd5-0a9021ce767a	476	assistant	agent	screen_analyzer	\N	**Problem:** Screen query returns only one top salary; I read the garbled question as "what if two employees tie for highest?" so return all of them.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\n1. Compute max(amount) as a scalar subquery\n2. Select emp_id, amount from Salary\n3. Filter rows where amount equals that max\n4. Execute and print all rows\n```\n\n**Code:**\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine, select, func\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase, Mapped, mapped_column, Session\n\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase): pass\n\nclass Salary(Base):\n    __tablename__ = "salary"\n    emp_id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    amount: Mapped[int]\n\nengine = create_engine("sqlite://")\nBase.metadata.create_all(engine)\n\nwith Session(engine) as s:\n    s.add_all([Salary(emp_id=1, amount=90), Salary(emp_id=2, amount=90),\n               Salary(emp_id=3, amount=70)])\n    s.commit()\n    top = select(func.max(Salary.amount)).scalar_subquery()\n    stmt = select(Salary.emp_id, Salary.amount).where(Salary.amount == top)\n    print(s.execute(stmt).all())  # [(1, 90), (2, 90)]\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Idea: Compare each row to the max, so ties all match instead of cutting with `limit(1)`.\n- Why correct: Every returned row has an amount equal to the table's maximum.\n- Edge cases: ties handled, empty table returns no rows (max is NULL), single row handled.\n- Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) extra space; sorting then limit is O(n log n).\n\n**Alternate method:** Your on-screen `order_by(desc).limit(1)` is O(n log n) and returns one row only. Also, use the class name `Salary` (not `salary`) and import `select`. Programiz may lack SQLAlchemy.	claude-sonnet-5-5	4745	1192	10769	{}	2026-10-29 07:11:53.905894+00	2026-10-29 07:11:55.183675+00
