2337ed7a-26dd-48bb-8f5e-6484bf1c02c0	03c687bf-0f6d-47ab-9f40-dcd4caa81525	91004547-4b32-4bab-85f4-8d98e71f1997	480	assistant	agent	conversor	\N	Q: Will the query work if two employees tie for the highest salary?\n\n**No, `limit(1)` returns only one of the tied employees, so I compare every row against the maximum salary instead.**\n\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import select, func\n\ntop = select(func.max(salary.amount)).scalar_subquery()\nstmt = select(salary.emp_id, salary.amount).where(salary.amount == top)\n\nprint(session.execute(stmt).all())  # amounts 90, 90, 70 -> [(1, 90), (2, 90)]\n```\n\n- The subquery finds the single highest amount, and the `where` filter returns every employee whose salary equals it.\n- Adding `distinct` on employee ID would not help, because `limit(1)` still cuts the result to one row.	claude-opus-5-5	8982	705	3283	{"roles": {"interviewee": "mic", "interviewer": "loopback"}}	2026-10-29 07:12:05.431005+00	2026-10-29 07:12:06.146751+00
