809552f6-9570-4bfd-8c7d-6847bb9d042e	67de7202-5b33-43b0-b70f-06323a6cc71e	65649d03-48b9-4ced-9c35-f3dc8d7def49	420	assistant	agent	conversor	\N	Q: Text-to-SQL vs Text2Cypher\n\n**Both turn a natural-language question into a database query, but Text-to-SQL targets relational tables while Text2Cypher targets a graph like Neo4j.**\n- For Text-to-SQL, the LLM gets the table and column schema, so it must work out the right joins itself.\n- For Text2Cypher, the LLM gets node labels, relationship types and directions, so it writes path patterns instead of joins.\n- Multi-hop questions, like supply-chain traceability across farms and mills, need many joins in SQL but stay as one short path pattern in Cypher.\n- LLMs usually write SQL more reliably, because Cypher errors on relationship direction are common, so I'd run either query read-only and validate it first.	claude-opus-5-5	9078	840	3637	{"roles": {"interviewee": "mic", "interviewer": "loopback"}}	2026-10-29 10:30:53.291026+00	2026-10-29 10:30:53.294609+00
